<h1>What is Configuration Management?</h1>

<p>
	Configuration management is a systematic aproach to managing,
	controlling and automating changes made to a (server) system over its
	entire lifecycle.
	It involves identifying, documenting, and verifying the various components
	that make up a system's configuration, as well as controlling how those
	components are modified and updated.
</p>

<p>
	The goals of configuration management vary based on your needs.
	Oftentimes motivations to introduce configuration management /
	Infrastructure as Code solutions include:
	<dl>
		<dt>Consistency</dt>
		<dd>
			Ensuring that all the components of a system work together
			seamlessly by controlling how changes are made.
			This reduces discrepancies between systems in an organisation which
			can lead to incompatibilities, unexpected behavior, or
			system failures.
		</dd>

		<dt>Traceability</dt>
		<dd>
			Maintaining a complete record of all the components
			that make up a system's configuration and how they relate to each
			other. This allows you to understand the current state of the
			system and how it has evolved over time.
			It can also allow you to revert to a previous configuration if
			necessary.
		</dd>

		<dt>Auditability</dt>
		<dd>
			Providing a clear history of all the changes made to a system,
			who made them, and when.
			This allows you to review the evolution of the system and
			troubleshoot issues.
		</dd>

		<dt>Reproducibility</dt>
		<dd>
			Enabling the ability to reliably recreate a specific configuration
			of a system, which is essential for tasks like testing, deployment,
			and disaster recovery.
		</dd>

		<dt>Automation</dt>
		<dd>
			Streamlining the configuration process through automation tools
			which can help reduce manual errors, save time, and improve
			efficiency.
		</dd>

		<dt>Compliance</dt>
		<dd>
			Ensuring that configurations meet regulatory and organizational
			standards, which is crucial for security and operational integrity.
		</dd>
	</dl>
</p>
